<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Not sandboxed: a global keyboard event tap and synthetic event posting
         are incompatible with the App Sandbox. Distribute via Developer ID,
         not the Mac App Store.

         The app runs under the Hardened Runtime (build.sh passes the
         "options runtime" flag to codesign), which is what stops another
         process running as the same user from loading its own code into a
         process that holds the Accessibility grant. Under the Hardened Runtime
         microphone access needs an entitlement in addition to the TCC prompt,
         so the one below is required for dictation to work at all.

         Note for editors: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so
         codesign flags are spelled out in words here. A stray one makes
         AMFIUnserializeXML fail and the bundle silently loses entitlements.

         Nothing else is requested: keystrokes go through a CGEventTap (gated by
         the Accessibility TCC grant), and the Apple-Events automation
         entitlement stays removed — nothing in the app sends Apple Events. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Library validation is disabled because it CANNOT work with a
         self-signed identity: it requires the process and every library it maps
         to share a Team ID, and a Team ID is only issued with a paid Apple
         Developer ID. Both this app and the bundled whisper.framework carry the
         same "Ghbdtn Local Signing" signature, yet dyld still refuses the
         framework with "different Team IDs" because neither has one.

         What is kept and what is given up, plainly:
         KEPT (this is why the Hardened Runtime is still on) - dyld ignores
         DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES and friends, unsigned code cannot be executed,
         and writable-executable memory is refused. That closes the cheap
         injection path, which needs no permission at all.
         GIVEN UP - a library that is signed by anyone at all may be loaded, so
         swapping the bundled framework for a signed malicious one is not
         blocked here. On macOS 14+ that still requires write access to the
         installed bundle, which is separately gated by App Management.
         Closing this last gap needs a Developer ID and nothing else. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
